# The production Android certificate is pinned

PRODUCTION-ANDROID-SIGNING-CERTIFICATE-PIN-1. Supersedes, forward and never by
rewriting, every earlier record that says the certificate is not pinned —
including rule 147 §3 and §6, which were accurate when written.

Rule 147 remains in force in full for the KEY: there is exactly one production
signer, it is permanent, and it must never be regenerated. This rule governs the
DECISION taken about it.

## 1. The trust anchor

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Alias | `daengtisia-clinic-release` |
| Algorithm | RSA 4096 |
| SHA-256 | `79db269b7cd38e920b80efbcf2f59142721f1e57924d3048d07a862f34fea2d9` |
| `signing.production_certificate_sha256` | **armed** with the value above |
| `signing.production_certificate_sha256_recorded` | the same value |

The fingerprint is **public** — it is derived from the certificate, which ships
inside every signed APK, so committing it discloses nothing. The private key,
the keystore and every passphrase remain out of git, off the production VPS and
out of CI. **No control in this subsystem may require the private key to
verify anything.** If an implementation appears to need it, that is an
architecture defect — stop and report it; it is never authority to open the
vault.

RECORDED and PINNED stay two fields answering two questions (rule 147 §3). Both
are now true. That is exactly when a split like this stops being scrutinised, so
they are asserted structurally — two keys, two summary lines, one certificate —
rather than by opposite values.

## 2. One semantic, asked in one place

`App\Support\Android\AndroidCertificateFingerprint` is the **only** definition of
what a fingerprint is and when two of them are the same.

1. **Reporting and enforcement must ask the same object.** The scanner's
   `production_certificate_pinned` summary and the verifier's
   `signer_trust_anchor` check both call `isValid()`. Never reintroduce a second
   opinion — an inline regex, a local `strtolower`, a `!== ''`. The predecessor
   shipped exactly that and the command printed `PRODUCTION_CERTIFICATE_PINNED=true`
   for a `'TBD'` the verifier was rejecting.
2. **`isPresent()` and `isValid()` are not the same predicate and must not be
   merged.** `isPresent()` asks "does the field carry a claim?" and the custody
   lifecycle needs it: a pin of `'TBD'` written while no key exists must still
   trip "a certificate is pinned while no key is claimed provisioned". Tightening
   it to `isValid()` makes that junk invisible to the rule that catches it — a
   fail-open regression that looks like a cleanup. A mutation test kills it.
3. **Accepted form is exactly 64 contiguous hex characters**, either case. The
   colon-separated form `keytool -list` prints is **rejected, never normalized**:
   a normalizer that strips separators also quietly accepts values a reviewer
   would reject. It fails closed and the operator reformats.
4. **The `D` regex modifier is load-bearing.** Without it PHP's `$` also matches
   before a trailing newline, so `"<64 hex>\n"` validated. That is not a
   whitespace nit: the anchor reported ARMED and the byte comparison then failed,
   printing "signed by a different key" — a newline in config reported to an
   operator as a **substituted signing key**.
5. **Compare case-insensitively, on the whole value.** `keytool` prints upper
   case, and a correct pin differing only in case must never be reported as a
   substitution. Never prefix, suffix, substring or `str_contains`. Invalid on
   either side is `false` — "could not tell" is never "matches".

## 3. Fail closed, and what that now means

`null`, `''`, whitespace, `'TBD'`, any placeholder, wrong length, non-hex,
embedded or trailing whitespace, colon form, SHA-1 length and any substituted
fingerprint are all **unpinned**, in the report and in the control alike. With
no valid anchor, `android:verify-release` authenticates nothing and says so.

`custody_state_machine_consistent` binds the pair in both directions: a pin
without a key, and a pin that disagrees with the recorded certificate, are
contradictions and go red.

## 4. Changing the pin is a signer rotation

Editing `production_certificate_sha256` to a different certificate is **not a
config tweak**. The key is unrecoverable and `app_signing_key_rotation` is
`constrained_treat_as_permanent`; a different anchor means every enrolled tablet
must be UNINSTALLED to take an update, which erases app data, destroys each
device's Keystore identity, and re-enrols the fleet by hand.

A different signer certificate must **never** be silently accepted. If the
fingerprint appears to have changed, that is a STOP condition under rule 147 §1.

## 5. Pinning is one decision, and it is not the other five

Arming the anchor moved exactly one thing. All of these remain false and are
asserted false:

- production APK built, signed or distributed; tablet touched; ADB used;
- device enrolled; pilot enforcement activated; global enforcement activated;
- `enforcement.active` false, `enforcement.current_stage` `off`,
  `owner_signoff.pilot_activated` false.

Reading `PRODUCTION_CERTIFICATE_PINNED=true` as permission to build, install or
enforce is the specific misreading this rule exists to prevent.

## 6. Gate hygiene learned here

1. **A containment check must assert what its own subject moved, not freeze a
   value another authority may lawfully change.** `preflight_unlocks_nothing`
   demanded the pin be `null` forever. The predecessor had already corrected the
   *message* on that exact line when the key was provisioned; the *condition*
   still read the pin, so this sprint hit the same wall one field later — and it
   would have reported a lawful pinning decision as "a real-device preflight pass
   unlocked signing", a false accusation on the message an operator acts on
   hardest.
2. **Replace such a predicate; do not delete it (rule 147 §5, Rule 147).** The
   clause is now `noUnauthorisedTrustAnchor()`: the pin must be **absent or
   exactly the recorded production certificate**. That is strictly narrower than
   "null" was broad, so a rogue well-formed anchor and junk both still go red.
   Two mutation tests exist solely to prove the gap was not orphaned.
3. **When a flag flips true, re-read every test that asserted it false.** Ten
   assertions across five suites pinned "not pinned" as a frozen value. Each was
   rewritten to its suite's own invariant — usually "this sprint introduced no
   anchor of its own", expressed as pin == recorded — instead of being deleted.
4. **A test that establishes its own precondition outlives the config.** The
   no-fallback proof ("the recorded fingerprint must not arm the verifier") only
   worked while the pin happened to be null. It now sets the pin to null itself,
   so the invariant stays testable for the life of the repository.
5. **Mutation-test the predicate you just documented.** The comment explaining
   why `isPresent()` must not become `isValid()` was written before any test
   enforced it, and the mutation survived. A claim in a comment that no test
   defends is exactly the drift this programme exists to catch.
6. **Register a new suite in `ci_runner.critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.** A
   filter token match is an accident of naming, and nothing would say the day it
   stopped selecting.
